1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,480
The personal protective equipment, PPE, worn by actors in this video, was selected based

2
00:00:05,480 --> 00:00:10,120
on a risk assessment of the chemicals used in the experiment, and conditions simulated

3
00:00:10,120 --> 00:00:11,560
in the activity.

4
00:00:11,560 --> 00:00:16,520
In scenes where the presenter did not wear PPE while in the lab, all chemicals were removed

5
00:00:16,520 --> 00:00:18,560
to eliminate chemical hazards.

6
00:00:18,560 --> 00:00:22,650
Hi, welcome to Chemistry Lab.

7
00:00:22,650 --> 00:00:25,890
If you're a college student, you may have already taken a high school chemistry course

8
00:00:25,890 --> 00:00:29,050
and learned some rules of lab safety along the way.

9
00:00:29,050 --> 00:00:32,450
As you begin college classes, you'll probably hear some of the same rules you learned in

10
00:00:32,450 --> 00:00:33,450
high school.

11
00:00:33,450 --> 00:00:37,490
In this video series, our goal is to build on those rules, but go a bit further.

12
00:00:37,490 --> 00:00:44,480
We'll establish basic principles of lab safety that you can use in any new situation.

13
00:00:44,480 --> 00:00:48,300
As you move through chemistry classes, you'll encounter many lab situations where general

14
00:00:48,300 --> 00:00:51,460
rules need some adjustment based on risk management.

15
00:00:51,460 --> 00:00:56,300
The steps for managing risks can easily be remembered using the acronym RAMP.

16
00:00:56,300 --> 00:01:04,300
RAMP stands for Recognize Hazards, Assess the Risk, Minimize the Risk, Prepare for Emergencies.

17
00:01:04,300 --> 00:01:06,540
Let's briefly take a look at each one.

18
00:01:06,540 --> 00:01:08,980
The first step is to recognize hazards.

19
00:01:08,980 --> 00:01:11,940
Simply put, a hazard is something that can harm you.

20
00:01:11,940 --> 00:01:16,140
In the Chemistry Lab, chemicals are an important source of hazards, but hazards can also arise

21
00:01:16,140 --> 00:01:18,620
from equipment and procedures.

22
00:01:18,620 --> 00:01:22,500
Sulfuric acid, a strong acid, is an example of a hazard.

23
00:01:22,500 --> 00:01:30,460
Sulfuric acid is useful because it reacts with many other chemicals.

24
00:01:30,460 --> 00:01:33,800
But it will also react with your skin, causing a chemical burn.

25
00:01:33,800 --> 00:01:37,300
The property that makes it useful also creates the hazard.

26
00:01:37,300 --> 00:01:40,820
In some experiments, it may be the procedure that is hazardous.

27
00:01:40,820 --> 00:01:44,820
If a solution needs to be heated, the source of heat creates the hazard.

28
00:01:44,820 --> 00:01:48,920
This is especially true if one of the chemical reagents is flammable.

29
00:01:48,920 --> 00:01:51,980
Step two of RAMP is about assessing risk.

30
00:01:51,980 --> 00:01:54,520
Risk is the chance of being harmed by a hazard.

31
00:01:54,520 --> 00:01:59,180
Even if a chemical is hazardous, it doesn't necessarily present a significant risk.

32
00:01:59,180 --> 00:02:03,220
Risk is a combination of both the severity of hazard and the likelihood of exposure.

33
00:02:03,220 --> 00:02:05,260
You can think of it like this.

34
00:02:05,260 --> 00:02:09,500
Risk equals severity of hazard times the likelihood of exposure.

35
00:02:09,500 --> 00:02:13,700
For example, a solution with a high concentration of nitric acid is very hazardous since it

36
00:02:13,700 --> 00:02:17,340
is both a strong acid and a good oxidizing agent.

37
00:02:17,340 --> 00:02:21,900
It's easy to control one drop, and if it got on your skin, it could be easily washed

38
00:02:21,900 --> 00:02:23,200
off.

39
00:02:23,200 --> 00:02:28,540
On the other hand, if you spill 50 milliliters of the solution on your leg, you get a lot

40
00:02:28,540 --> 00:02:32,120
of exposure, and it could cause significant damage.

41
00:02:32,120 --> 00:02:36,980
You can see that the amount of the acid in the way it's used affects the level of risk.

42
00:02:36,980 --> 00:02:40,900
Another example would be the use of a liquid like ethyl ether where the vapor is unsafe

43
00:02:40,900 --> 00:02:42,720
to breathe.

44
00:02:42,720 --> 00:02:46,440
Using this in the open part of the lab presents significant risk.

45
00:02:46,440 --> 00:02:50,380
Using it in a chemical hood is much safer since the likelihood of inhalation is greatly

46
00:02:50,380 --> 00:02:51,380
reduced.

47
00:02:51,380 --> 00:02:55,580
We'll dig deeper into assessing risk in another video, but for now, just remember

48
00:02:55,580 --> 00:02:59,940
all experiments pose some risk, and most experiments can be done safely if the risk

49
00:02:59,940 --> 00:03:02,180
is reduced to acceptable levels.

50
00:03:02,180 --> 00:03:05,740
The third step in RAMP is to minimize risk from the hazards.

51
00:03:05,740 --> 00:03:08,660
This is the origin of many familiar lab rules.

52
00:03:08,660 --> 00:03:14,840
For example, working in a chemical hood to avoid breathing hazardous gases and vapors,

53
00:03:14,840 --> 00:03:19,820
wearing eye protection to avoid any possibility of corrosive substances damaging sensitive

54
00:03:19,820 --> 00:03:24,120
eye tissue, and wearing gloves and or a lab coat when a procedure may expose skin to

55
00:03:24,120 --> 00:03:25,120
potential injury.

56
00:03:25,120 --> 00:03:28,800
Here are some other questions you can ask to help minimize exposure.

57
00:03:28,800 --> 00:03:32,920
Can you use a less hazardous chemical in place of a more hazardous one?

58
00:03:32,920 --> 00:03:36,900
Can you use lower concentrations of chemicals?

59
00:03:36,900 --> 00:03:40,580
Can you run a reaction with smaller amounts of chemicals?

60
00:03:40,580 --> 00:03:44,200
Can you lower the temperature to reduce the risk of burns?

61
00:03:44,200 --> 00:03:48,480
It's not possible to remove all risk, but risk can and should always be minimized.

62
00:03:48,480 --> 00:03:52,600
Check out the other two videos in this series that discuss minimizing risk more thoroughly.

63
00:03:52,600 --> 00:03:55,400
The final step in RAMP is to prepare for emergencies.

64
00:03:55,400 --> 00:03:59,080
If something goes wrong, are you prepared?

65
00:03:59,080 --> 00:04:01,980
Do you know how to handle a fire or respond to a spill?

66
00:04:01,980 --> 00:04:05,720
We go over these scenarios in more detail in other videos, but just remember that you

67
00:04:05,720 --> 00:04:09,680
should consciously think about what might go wrong in an experiment and then be ready

68
00:04:09,680 --> 00:04:13,280
to respond.

69
00:04:13,280 --> 00:04:17,200
The safety knowledge and skills that you learn in your chemistry classes are greatly influenced

70
00:04:17,200 --> 00:04:19,200
by the culture of your institution.

71
00:04:19,200 --> 00:04:22,520
As you start working in the lab, you should think about how you can contribute to this

72
00:04:22,520 --> 00:04:23,520
culture.

73
00:04:23,520 --> 00:04:26,880
A positive culture of safety requires you to do your part.

74
00:04:26,880 --> 00:04:32,480
This starts with the right attitude and ethics, value safety, work safely, don't engage in

75
00:04:32,480 --> 00:04:37,520
risky behavior, promote safety, and accept responsibility for safety.

76
00:04:37,520 --> 00:04:42,100
Everyone using chemicals in their career needs the right knowledge, skills, and attitudes

77
00:04:42,100 --> 00:04:43,960
to work safely in the lab.

78
00:04:43,960 --> 00:04:47,320
Keep safety at the forefront in your chemistry and science education and it will serve

79
00:04:47,320 --> 00:04:49,920
you well.

80
00:04:49,920 --> 00:04:53,800
As you can see, there's a lot more to learn about safety than just following rules.

81
00:04:53,800 --> 00:04:56,400
What are the hazards an experiment can present?

82
00:04:56,400 --> 00:04:57,920
How do we learn about those hazards?

83
00:04:57,920 --> 00:05:02,740
How do we decide which hazards really present some risks to you and how serious is the risk?

84
00:05:02,740 --> 00:05:06,160
For a deeper dive into these topics, be sure to check out the other five videos in this

85
00:05:06,160 --> 00:05:07,160
series.

86
00:05:07,160 --> 00:05:11,240
You can find them by clicking here, checking out the links in the description, or visiting

87
00:05:11,240 --> 00:05:13,640
acs.org slash safety videos.

88
00:05:13,640 --> 00:05:26,920
Thanks for watching!
